\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

Projects the reliability of a fitted \code{\link[=icc]{icc()}} to the mean of \code{m} raters, for
any \code{m}. It reuses the fit's variance components, each a share of the total
variation traced to one source. This is a generalizability-theory \strong{decision
study} (D-study), which projects the fitted variance components to other
rater counts. It answers "how reliable would the mean of \code{m} raters be?"
and, read as a curve, "how many raters do I need?". The point estimate and
its interval reuse the fit stored on \code{x}, and no model is refit. The
interval is boundary-aware, since an estimate can land exactly at zero. The
band follows the fit's \code{ci_method}. A Monte-Carlo fit, whose interval is
built by simulating from the fitted model, reprojects one draw from the
parameter covariance across every \code{m}. A \strong{bootstrap} fit, which refits the
model on simulated data many times, reprojects its stored resamples. So on
a bootstrap fit, at \code{m} = the observed rater count the band matches the
fitted \verb{ICC(*,k)} interval exactly.
}
\section{Projection is extrapolation}{

Projecting to an \code{m} you did not run is an \strong{extrapolation}. Its
trustworthiness depends on how well the variance components are pinned
down. The rater variance \eqn{\sigma^2_r} matters most, since it is
estimated from only as many raters as you observed. With few raters that
estimate is noisy, so the projected interval is honestly wide. The
Monte-Carlo interval widens automatically, recomputing \eqn{\Phi(m)} on
every draw rather than pretending a single plugged-in value. \code{m} is the
number of raters and is normally an integer, though non-integer values are
permitted.

Projection is defined for random raters, under both error definitions.
Those are agreement, where raters give the same score, and consistency,
where raters agree apart from a constant offset per rater. It is also
defined for fixed-rater \strong{consistency}, fixed meaning the observed raters
are the whole population of interest. In a two-way design the subjects
share one set of raters. The \strong{one-way} model, where they need not, is
covered as a Spearman-Brown projection of \code{ICC(1)}. Projection is \strong{not}
defined for fixed-rater absolute agreement. There the rater term is the
finite-population variance, the spread of just the observed raters. So
there is no "average of \code{m} freshly sampled raters" to project to.
\code{d_study()} aborts in that case (use \code{raters = "random"}).
}

\section{Multilevel projections}{

For a multilevel fit (a \code{cluster} column), \code{d_study()} projects the rater
count \code{m} for each correctly-partitioned level on the object. Those are the
\strong{subject} and/or \strong{cluster} level, the cluster level being how reliably
raters distinguish cluster means. It returns one reliability curve per
level, and the returned object gains a \code{level} column that \code{autoplot()}
facets by. \code{tidy()} carries that column on every projection, \code{NA} where the
fit is not multilevel.
This is the paper-sanctioned rater projection (ten Hove et al. 2022). Here
\code{m} is the number of raters per cluster. The cluster-level coefficient does
\strong{not} average over subjects, so there is no "subjects per cluster"
projection. That is a sample-size question, not a reliability one. Nested
designs project the subject level only. The conflated diagnostic
(\code{level = "conflated"}), the single-level ICC that ignores clustering, is
not projected. On \strong{incomplete} data the \strong{subject} level projects,
because projection moves only the divisor. On that same data the
\strong{cluster} level is dropped with a note. Projecting \code{m} raters there is the
averaged \code{ICC(c,k)} case, whose ragged divisor is an open modeling question.
}

\section{Within-cell replicate fits}{

A replicate is one of several ratings by the same rater of the same subject.
In a within-cell replicate fit (more than one rating per subject-by-rater
cell) the residual splits into the subject-by-rater interaction and pure
error. On such a fit \code{d_study()} can project \strong{either} axis (one per call):
\itemize{
\item the \strong{rater count \code{m}} (the default), holding the occasion count fixed.
The rater and interaction terms divide by \code{m}, and pure error by \code{m} times
the curve's own occasion setting. The returned object gains an \code{occasions}
column, one reliability curve per distinct value that column holds on the
fit. A single-occasion setting divides pure error by \code{m} alone, an
occasion-averaged one by \code{m} times the fitted occasion count. At \code{m} = the
observed rater count each curve matches the fitted \verb{ICC(*,k)} for its own
level and occasion setting, where the fit reports one. Where the fit
reports a cluster level, its \code{occasions} column also carries that level's
placeholder 1. That error set has no pure error to average. So such a
fit made with \code{occasions = "average"} alone still projects a subject curve
at 1, which the fit itself does not report.
\code{tidy()} carries that column on every projection, \code{NA} where the fit has no
replicates.
\item the \strong{swept occasion count \code{n_o}} (supply the \code{n_o} argument), holding
raters at the observed count. Pure error divides by \code{m * n_o} while the
rater and interaction terms are unchanged. Because occasion averaging
rescales \strong{only pure error}, this curve is well-posed for random \strong{and}
fixed raters. That includes fixed absolute agreement, which the rater
projection refuses, since occasions are a random facet however the raters
are treated. Where the swept \code{n_o} equals the fitted occasion count it
matches the fitted \verb{ICC(*,k)}.
}

\strong{The occasion curve has a finite ceiling.} As \code{n_o} grows it approaches
\verb{sigma^2_s / (sigma^2_s + (sigma^2_r + sigma^2_sr) / m)}, \strong{not} 1.
Averaging more occasions washes out only pure measurement error, never the
rater or subject-by-rater variance. Read it as "how much does re-rating help?", which
plateaus, unlike adding raters.

Take a multilevel replicate fit (crossed Design 1 or nested Design 2). A
\strong{rater} projection moves the subject level across occasion settings and
the cluster level single-occasion. An \strong{occasion} projection moves the
subject level across \code{n_o} and returns the cluster level as a \strong{flat}
curve. The cluster-level error set (\verb{\{rater, cluster:rater\}}) has no
pure-error term, so averaging occasions cannot change it, and \code{d_study()}
notes this. \strong{Ragged} replicate fits are refused for either axis (the
occasion-averaged ragged divisor is an open modeling question).
}

